/* ==============================================================
   cold_start — guarantee the robot behaves the way you expect
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   PURPOSE   Wipe every leftover setting, so a demo behaves the
             same on a robot someone else has been fiddling with
   SAVE AS   lib/cold_start
   CALL      StartProgram("lib/cold_start")
   NEEDS     Robot powered on, connected in CONTROL mode
   MOVES     Nothing. See the note on homing below.
   TIME      ~10 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE PROBLEM

   You arrive at a customer site. The robot on the bench was last
   used by someone else. Did they leave a tool frame defined? A
   restricted joint limit? A work zone? Simulation mode on?

   Any one of those makes your demo behave differently from the
   way it behaved when you rehearsed it, and you will not know
   why while a customer is watching.

   THE TWO KINDS OF SETTING

   This is the distinction that makes the whole thing tractable,
   and it is worth knowing cold.

   1. NON-PERSISTENT settings live only for as long as the robot
      is activated. TRF, WRF, speeds, blending, payload, posture
      and turn selection, torque limits, move mode. The manual
      puts it plainly: by deactivating the robot, you lose all
      settings that are not persistent.

      So DEACTIVATE + REACTIVATE resets all of them, in one
      stroke, back to firmware defaults. That is Part 2 below.

   2. PERSISTENT settings are written to the robot's SD card and
      survive deactivation, reboots, and power cycles. Only 13
      commands set these, and deactivating does NOTHING to them.

      These are the ones that will surprise you, because there is
      no natural moment at which they get cleared. They have to
      be dealt with deliberately. That is Parts 1 and 3.

   THE HAPPY ACCIDENT

   Every persistent setting can only be changed while the robot
   is DEACTIVATED. Which means the deactivate step you need
   anyway is exactly the window in which you can fix them. That
   is why this program is shaped the way it is.

   ® DOES THIS MOVE THE ROBOT?

   No. Deactivating a Meca500 that was already homed and then
   reactivating it does not require homing again, so the arm does
   not move.

   The one exception: if an MEGP 25* gripper is installed, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. Even then, only the GRIPPER
   homes — the robot itself still does not move. Make sure the
   fingers are clear.

   (This is unlike basic/01, which calls Home() on a robot that
   may never have been homed since power-on. That one does move.)
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ============================================================
   PART 1 — AUDIT: see what you are dealing with
   ============================================================

   Run this part FIRST, on its own, and read the log panel.
   Nothing here changes anything — these are all data requests.

   This is the safe answer to "is this robot set up the way I
   expect?", and on a customer's own robot it is often the only
   part of this file you should run. Look before you reset. */

GetFwVersion()              // are the examples written for this firmware?
GetProductType()            // MUST be a Meca500 — see the note below
GetRobotSerial()

GetJointLimitsCfg()         // [2094][1] means USER JOINT LIMITS ARE ACTIVE
GetWorkZoneLimits()         // a cuboid tighter than +/-10000 is configured
GetWorkZoneCfg()            // severity and what gets checked
GetToolSphere()             // non-zero radius means a tool is declared
GetCollisionCfg()
GetSimModeCfg()             // which sim mode ActivateSim() would pick

GetStatusRobot()            // activated? homed? in error? in sim mode?

/* Reading the answers:

     GetJointLimitsCfg  -> [2094][0]  factory limits          GOOD
                           [2094][1]  someone restricted a joint
     GetWorkZoneLimits  -> +/-10000 on every axis             GOOD
                           anything tighter is a configured cage
     GetToolSphere      -> [0, 0, 0, 0]  no tool declared     GOOD
     GetCollisionCfg    -> 4  factory default                 GOOD
     GetSimModeCfg      -> 1  normal (real-time) simulation   GOOD

   If all of those read as GOOD, you do not need Part 3 at all —
   Part 2 alone will give you a clean robot.

   Á CHECK GetProductType FIRST. This repo is written for the
     Meca500 and nothing else. Every example assumes six-axis
     geometry — six-value joint sets, the Meca500's joint limits,
     three-parameter posture configuration. On any other
     Mecademic model those assumptions are wrong, and the
     programs will either be refused or move somewhere you did
     not intend. If this is not a Meca500, stop here. */


/* ============================================================
   PART 2 — THE RESET THAT IS ALWAYS SAFE
   ============================================================

   Deactivate, then reactivate. Everything non-persistent goes
   back to its firmware default: TRF and WRF back to the flange
   and the base, speeds back to 25% and 150 mm/s, blending back
   to 100%, payload back to 0, torque limits back to 100%,
   posture and turn selection back to automatic, move mode back
   to velocity-based.

   No editing required, nothing cell-specific, nothing
   destructive. If you only run one part of this file, run this
   one. */

DeactivateRobot()

/* --- the robot is now deactivated -----------------------------
   This is the ONLY window in which Part 3 can run. If you want
   it, uncomment it now — it has to sit here, between the
   deactivate and the activate. */


/* ============================================================
   PART 3 — PERSISTENT SETTINGS  (opt-in; read the warning)
   ============================================================

   Á WARNING — DO NOT UNCOMMENT THIS ON A CUSTOMER'S PRODUCTION
     ROBOT WITHOUT ASKING.

     These lines return the persistent settings to their FACTORY
     defaults. On a bare demo robot that is exactly what you
     want. On a robot in a real cell, several of them are the
     safety envelope somebody configured deliberately — a work
     zone that stops the arm reaching the loading window, a joint
     limit protecting a cable. Resetting those silently removes
     protection that the cell was built around.

     Rule of thumb: uncomment on a demo or loaner robot. On a
     customer's own robot, run Part 1, show them what it says,
     and let them decide.

   These are commented out deliberately. Uncomment as a block. */

// SetJointLimitsCfg(0)                    // factory joint limits
// SetWorkZoneLimits(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)     // the zeros form resets to factory
// SetWorkZoneCfg(4, 1)                    // factory: error on breach, check FCP
// SetToolSphere(0, 0, 0, 0)               // no tool declared
// SetCollisionCfg(4)                      // factory: warn and raise a motion error
// SetCalibrationCfg(1)                    // factory: calibration ENABLED
// SetSimModeCfg(1)                        // factory: normal real-time sim

/* Notes on three of those:

   SetJointLimitsCfg(0) DISABLES user-defined limits and restores
   the factory ones. It does not erase the user limits — they
   stay in memory and can be re-enabled later with
   SetJointLimitsCfg(1). Nothing is lost.

   SetCalibrationCfg(1) matters more than it looks. With
   calibration disabled, Cartesian moves go to slightly different
   poses — MoveJoints is unaffected, MovePose and MoveLin are
   not. A robot with calibration switched off is a robot whose
   accuracy quietly does not match the datasheet.

   SetWorkZoneLimits(0,0,0,0,0,0) is the documented reset form.
   All-zeros means "factory default", which is +/-10,000 mm on
   every axis — effectively no work zone at all.

   NOT TOUCHED HERE, ON PURPOSE:

     SetRobotName, SetNetworkOptions, EnableEtherNetIp,
     EnableProfinet

   Those are persistent too, but they are the robot's identity
   and how it is wired into the customer's network. Resetting
   them from a demo script would be rude at best and would take
   the robot off the network at worst.

     SetOfflineProgramLoop

   Also persistent, and it decides whether the button on the
   robot base loops program "1". It cannot go in this file
   anyway: the robot only accepts it while a program is being
   saved with StartSaving. Set it from the MecaPortal.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ============================================================
   PART 4 — BACK TO READY FOR MOTION
   ============================================================

   The same four commands as basic/01, and for the same reasons.
   All idempotent, so none of them fails if there is nothing to
   do. */

ActivateRobot()

/* Home() is a no-op here on a robot that was already homed, so
   the arm does not move. It is included because it is required
   if the robot was NOT homed before this ran, and because with a
   gripper installed it re-homes the gripper. */
Home()

ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


/* ============================================================
   PART 5 — THE HOUSE SETTINGS
   ============================================================

   Part 2 left every non-persistent setting at its FIRMWARE
   default. That is a known state, but it is not necessarily
   OUR known state — this repo runs slower than the firmware
   default, and a real cell has a tool frame.

   So finish by applying the house settings. Save
   common/init.mxprog as "lib/init" first, or comment this out. */

StartProgram("lib/init")

LogTrace("lib/cold_start complete")
SetCheckpoint(99)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   HOW TO USE THIS IN PRACTICE

   Before a demo, on a robot you do not know:
     1. Run Part 1 alone. Select those lines, press Ctrl+Enter,
        read the log.
     2. If anything reads as non-factory, decide whether it is
        deliberate. Ask, if it is not your robot.
     3. Run the whole file.

   On your own demo robot: just run the whole file, with Part 3
   uncommented. Ten seconds, and the robot is in a state you can
   predict.

   ® ONE THING TO VERIFY ON HARDWARE

   Deactivating from inside a running program is legal — the
   commands allow it, and every usage restriction here is
   satisfied in this order. But a deactivate clears the motion
   queue, and this repo was written without a robot to hand, so
   the MecaPortal's exact behaviour when its program runner meets
   a DeactivateRobot mid-file has not been confirmed.

   If the run stops at Part 2 rather than continuing, split this
   file in three and run them in order, or do the deactivate and
   reactivate with the MecaPortal toolbar buttons and run only
   Parts 3 and 5. The command sequence is correct either way.
   Please delete this note once someone has run it on a robot.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
